Implement user story MOCK3-2: The departure board omits the final stop

## Story Description
formatStops renders every stop except the last one, so a three-stop line shows two. The journey length reported by totalMinutes is correct, which is why the omission has gone unnoticed.

## Acceptance Criteria
- Every stop on the line appears on the rendered board, including the final one.
- Stops remain separated by the existing arrow separator.
- An empty line still renders without error.

## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.

_(fix-plan, from: code-graph-detective)_

- **src/schedule.ts** (`formatStops`): The for-loop bound `i < stops.length - 1` excludes the last index, so the final stop is never pushed into parts. Changing the bound to `i < stops.length` makes every stop, including the last, appear in the rendered string. The separator `' -> '` already exists on line 9 and is reused unchanged.  <== **THIS CHANGE PRODUCES THE VALUE** — it is what makes the new or corrected data exist. Without it, every other change here only moves a value that never changed.
  - **Minimal fix:** Change the loop bound on line 6 from `i < stops.length - 1` to `i < stops.length`. No other changes needed ? the template literal on line 7 and the `' -> '` join on line 9 already handle the last stop correctly once the loop visits it.
- **test/schedule.test.ts** (`schedule.test.ts`): Existing tests only assert that the output contains '->' and 'Union'; none check the last stop ('Eglinton'). Once formatStops is fixed, a test must verify the final stop name appears in the rendered string, and that an empty line still renders without error (matching the acceptance criteria).  (verify only — no edit expected here)
  - **Minimal fix:** Add an assertion that formatStops(LINE) contains 'Eglinton' (the last stop in the 3-stop test data), and an assertion for the empty-line case (formatStops([]) returns ''). The existing LINE constant and the formatStops import are reused.



## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- The string returned by formatStops contains the name of the final stop in the input array.
- The string returned by formatStops contains the ' -> ' separator between stop entries.
- Calling formatStops with an empty array returns a string without throwing an error.

